+# port-check
+
+Fast TCP port checker for the command line. Check if ports are open on remote hosts with support for port ranges, parallel scanning, and JSON output.
+
+## Features
+
+- Check single ports, comma-separated lists, or ranges (e.g. `1-1024`)
+- Parallel connection checking with configurable concurrency
+- Configurable timeout
+- JSON output for scripting and automation
+- Exit code 1 when all ports are closed (useful for health checks)
+- DNS resolution with IP address fallback
+
+## Install
+
+```bash
+go install github.com/TataneSan/port-check@latest
+```
+
+Or build from source:
+
+```bash
+git clone https://github.com/TataneSan/port-check.git
+cd port-check
+go build -o port-check main.go
+```
+
+## Usage
+
+```
+port-check [options] <host> <port>[,port,...]
+```
+
+### Options
+
+| Flag | Default | Description |
+|------|---------|-------------|
+| `-t` | `3s` | Connection timeout (e.g. `5s`, `100ms`) |
+| `-json` | `false` | Output results as JSON |
+| `-p` | `10` | Max parallel connections |
+
+### Examples
+
+Check a single port:
+
+```bash
+port-check google.com 443
+```
+
+Check multiple ports:
+
+```bash
+port-check example.com 22,80,443,8080
+```
+
+Scan a port range:
+
+```bash
+port-check 192.168.1.1 1-1024 -p 50
+```
+
+JSON output:
+
+```bash
+port-check -json example.com 80,443
+```
+
+Custom timeout:
+
+```bash
+port-check -t 5s slow-server.com 80
+```
+
+### Output
+
+Table format (default):
+
+```
